 **B141. THE DESCRIPTOR RULE HE HAS NOW ASKED FOR TWICE, AND 9 GUARD FILES THAT WERE OFF THE COMMIT GATE.** Added 2026-11-10T17:28:24+00:00. **HIS WORDS, 2026-11-10, verbatim:** *"I can't always tell when you print identifiers like B102 or C0003 (for example), if these are the same issues you may have been discussing a few lines ago. I don't have access to your ticket store, or any of the other resources you have to store these issues. Nor do I have your ability to quickly look them up. I already asked you when you print these labels, to include a short descriptor, so I can know what you are talking about."* **THE RULE ALREADY EXISTED AND ITS SCOPE WAS THE DEFECT.** Note standard Rule 32 says exactly this and `scripts/note_vagueness_lint.py` already implements the predicate, but Rule 32 is scoped to delivered DOCUMENTS, because unscoped it fires on 412 of 1,187 notes. Chat was never in scope, so the rule had never once been applied to the surface he reads. **MEASURED BEFORE ANYTHING WAS BUILT, by running that same predicate over every assistant text block in the session transcripts: 8288 of 10496 entry codes printed with NO descriptor, 0.789634, Wilson [0.781732, 0.797325], Clopper-Pearson [0.781710, 0.797398]**, both intervals on 2 routes agreeing to 2.220e-16 -- the Clopper-Pearson second route computed from the EXACT BINOMIAL TAILS, because mpmath's `betainc` fails to converge at these parameters. Worst offenders B20 at 739 bare, B21 at 626, B22 at 281. **The figure is an UPPER bound and is labelled so**: the predicate demands a content word from the entry's own title in the same sentence, so a sentence naming the subject in different words is still counted. **THE FIX IS AVAILABILITY, NOT RESOLVE.** The descriptor was missing because fetching it cost a tool call. `scripts/task_list_markers.py:open_roster` emits every OPEN entry as `CODE descriptor` and `hooks/task_list_pulse.py` injects it into EVERY turn beside the existing line, at about 863 characters, so printing one now costs nothing. His own `no-word-numbers` ruling settles what a restated rule would have been worth: *"The failure was never the absence of a rule or a checker; it was never running the checker."* **AND THE SECOND HALF: 9 GUARD FILES WERE RUNNING NOWHERE.** 5 written during the night of 2026-11-09 to 2026-11-10 were committed without being added to `hooks/pre-commit`, so each guarded a repair the gate could not catch regressing; 3 more were already on the gate and UNDECLARED, so the cost pin had been stale by 3 files independently of tonight -- 3 of 235, 0.012766, Wilson [0.004351, 0.036854]. The cause is exact: `bench/tests/test_precommit_gate_cost_2026-09-10.py` appears 0 times in `hooks/pre-commit`, so the guard whose whole job is to compare the declared cost against what the gate collects is itself outside the board it measures, and its assertion is unconditional with no skip. The gate now runs 243 files and the pin is refreshed. **[NEEDS HIS RULING] on whether that cost guard should go ON the gate**, split so the FILE-COUNT half gates every commit -- it only moves on a deliberate edit of the guard list -- while the COLLECTED-COUNT half, which moves whenever an entry closes, stays in the full suite. Producers: `scripts/a_bare_entry_code_in_chat_2026-11-10.py` and `scripts/precommit_gate_cost_2026-09-10.py`. ★★★ **AND THE SAME GUARD HAS BEEN OUTGROWN RATHER THAN BROKEN, MEASURED 2026-11-11, WHICH STRENGTHENS THE CASE FOR THE SPLIT ABOVE.** Its `test_the_script_exits_zero` runs the board end to end under a TYPED 1800 s subprocess budget, and the board has outgrown it. Read off `.cdsfl_tmp/precommit_durations.jsonl`, excluding rows under 300 s because an infrastructure refusal is not an observation of what a board costs: the board **FIRST crossed 1800 s on 2026-11-09 at 22:47:27, at 1922 s**, which is BEFORE any of the 2026-11-10 work, so the 2 guard files added that night are not the cause — they are about 50 tests of 4348. **10 of 45 full boards are over the budget, 0.222222, Wilson 95% [0.125447, 0.362693]**, and the median full board is **1666 s, 4.98 times the 334.83 s the guard's own comment quoted as current**. **THE WORSE PROPERTY IS THAT IT FLAPPED RATHER THAN FAILED**: it passed at 1791 s and 1798 s and failed at 1817 s, 1845 s and 1969 s, so the verdict tracked machine load rather than the gate, which is how a checker comes to be read as noise. **FIXED by DERIVING the budget from the ledger** — `max(1800, 2 x the worst of the last 10 recorded full boards)`, today 3938 s — the same pattern `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py:commit_timeout` already uses and defends, with the old value kept as a FLOOR so the bound can only widen and never tighten. It still catches a genuine hang, which is the only reason to have a bound, because it now scales with what the board actually costs. **AND THE STALE 334.83 s FIGURE IN THE GUARD'S OWN PROSE IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN LEFT**, under `docs-defect-parity`. **NOT VERIFIED END TO END YET, AND THAT IS STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED:** the guard is on 0 lines of `hooks/pre-commit`, so the commit board cannot exercise it, and the 1 test that does costs a full board to run. The 11 cheap tests in the file pass and the derivation is verified by execution; the expensive test is owed a run. Producer for these figures: the ledger itself, read by `scripts/what_the_commit_gate_actually_costs_over_time_2026-11-08.py`. Guards: `bench/tests/test_an_entry_code_never_travels_alone_in_chat_2026-11-10.py`, 13 tests, 6 of 6 mutations red; and `bench/tests/test_a_mid_turn_message_is_still_his_2026-11-10.py`, 21 tests, 7 of 7 mutations red, for the Stop-gate predicate that could not see a message he sends mid-turn.
