-**B193. `sv` APPENDS A CO-AUTHOR TRAILER THE MESSAGE ALREADY CARRIES, SO FOLLOWING THE HARNESS'S OWN ATTRIBUTION RULE STAMPS EVERY COMMIT TWICE.** Added 2026-11-14T00:45:00+00:00. **STATUS: BUILT, TESTED, 6 OF 6 MUTATIONS RED, ON THE GATE. MINE. NO RULING IS REQUESTED.** **FIND, with a live instance rather than a supposition.** `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py:1214` read `full_msg = f"{message}\n\n{trailer}"` with no check for a trailer the message already ends with. The harness's attribution rule instructs the author to END a commit message with `Co-Authored-By: ...`, so obeying that rule and committing through `sv` produced the trailer TWICE. **Commit `4a802768` of 2026-11-14 carries 2 identical trailers**, read back with `git log -1 --format=%B | grep -c`, which is how this was found. **FOLLOW, traced before the edit.** `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py:1220` is the ONLY production `git commit -m` that builds a message; every other hit in the tree is a test fixture. `_co_author_trailer()` has 1 production call site and 4 assertions in `bench/tests/test_cdsfl_sv_repairs.py` at lines 121, 129, 142 and 150, all of which test the trailer's VALUE rather than its appending, so none is affected. `bench/tests/test_ffafp_audit_hook_2026-09-05.py:575` mentions the trailer only inside a fixture string. **ANALYSE, and the obvious repair is the wrong one.** A substring test — `trailer in message` — fails in the direction that SUPPRESSES a real trailer: a commit message whose BODY quotes `Co-Authored-By:`, for example one describing this very defect, would match and the commit would then carry no attribution at all. **Losing an attribution is a provenance failure; duplicating a trailer is cosmetic**, so the asymmetry decides the design. **FIX.** `_append_co_author(message, trailer)` compares TRAILERS, not substrings: it takes the message's LAST paragraph, treats it as a trailer block only when every non-empty line in it is trailer-shaped (`^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*:\s`), and appends unless the exact trailer is already a line in that block. **P-PASS: 12 tests EXECUTING the function, and the mutation harness found a case I had not written.** Loosening the trailer-shape pattern to match any line left the guard GREEN on the first pass, which means nothing was testing what that pattern is for. The missing case is a last paragraph that is PROSE but carries the trailer on a line of its own: without the shape requirement that reads as a trailer block, the dedupe fires, and the commit loses its attribution entirely. Added, and **6 of 6 mutations now turn the guard red, 1.000000, Wilson [0.609666, 1.000000], Clopper-Pearson [0.540742, 1.000000]**, statsmodels against a hand-rolled Wilson and against `scipy.stats.beta` agreeing to 0.000e+00, with a third mpmath route from the exact binomial tails returning the same 0.540742. **AND 2 OF MY OWN 6 FIRST EXPECTATIONS WERE WRONG**, which is recorded rather than quietly fixed under the project's rule that an anchor is derived from the subject and an expectation never is: the body-mention case was asserted at 1 occurrence when the stated design makes it 2 — 1 quoted plus 1 appended — and the different-trailer case was asserted at 2 when its input contains the co-author trailer 0 times. The function was right both times. Guard: `bench/tests/test_the_co_author_trailer_is_not_doubled_2026-11-14.py`, 12 tests, on `hooks/pre-commit`. Harness: `scripts/the_co_author_dedupe_mutations_2026-11-14.py`, which verifies every mutation changed the file and restores the subject from bytes captured before the first one. **THE DUPLICATE IN `4a802768` IS LEFT WHERE IT IS**: amending it would re-run the board for a cosmetic repair, and the trailer is not false — the work is co-authored. Tracked as `pf:2984bafcec8b`, the duplicated co-author trailer.
