+    "title": "B144 - the critical cut of 0.7 is not a severity measurement, because nothing it decides survives a change of units",
+    "spoken_heading": "WHETHER EACH DOMAIN DECLARES ITS OWN SEVERITY SCALE AND ITS OWN CRITICAL LEVEL, AND WHETHER A RUN RECORDS WHICH DOMAIN IT USED",
+    "what": "You asked on the 11th of November whether pinning an exact number on severity is an arbitrary and vacuous exercise, once severity is accepted as a quantity that each branch of science already measures on its own scale. The measurement agrees with you. A gate needs 2 things from a domain, not 1: the ordering of consequences, and a marked point on that ordering. Rank every defect from least bad to worst and you still have no idea where bad enough to stop begins, because the ranking alone does not contain that information. Real fields supply the marked point: software severity scoring calls it High, clinical grading calls it Grade 3, the nuclear event scale calls it level 4. The project's 0.7 is not such a point.",
+    "why": "Two independent results, and the second is the one that settles it. First, a constraint solver confirms that a re-scaling which preserves the order of every single pair can still change how many findings sit above a fixed value of 0.7, while the same attack on a cut named by a marked point of the ordering has no solution at all. Second, on the archive's own 3,170 recorded severities, multiplying every one of them by 0.6993 - which reorders nothing, with a rank correlation of exactly 1 and an exhaustive pairwise check clean - destroys 1,416 of 1,416 critical calls, the whole of them. So every critical judgement the project has ever made can be erased by a change of units that alters no ordering. A free panel round measured the same thing from the other side: carried linearly into the 4 validated scales, 0.7 lands exactly on a recognised boundary in 1 of them and between levels in the other 3. And a third part of the finding is sharper still, because 174 of those 1,416 critical calls are decided by a tie at a boundary whose value is not even 0.7 in the real numbers.",
     "evidence_line": "By syntax tree on the reference runner: 1 command-line domain argument defaulting to software, 0 functions that classify a domain from task content, and 0 of 67 archived run states recording any domain at any depth. 0 of 20 domain configuration files declare a severity scale or a critical level, Wilson [0.000000, 0.161125], Clopper-Pearson [0.000000, 0.168433]. On the archive, 1,416 of 1,416 critical calls destroyed by an order-preserving re-scaling, 1.000000, Wilson [0.997294, 1.000000], Clopper-Pearson [0.997398, 1.000000], counted on 2 independent routes that agree exactly. 174 of 1,416 sit exactly on the cut, 0.122881, Wilson [0.106795, 0.141009]. Verified on 6 routes: SymPy, z3, exact rational arithmetic, mpmath, NumPy and Wolfram Language.",
     "options": [
       "DECLARE THE SCALE AND THE CRITICAL LEVEL IN EACH DOMAIN CONFIGURATION, AND RECORD THE RESOLVED DOMAIN IN THE RUN STATE. This is the recommendation. Neither half removes the existing cut or changes what the gate admits today; they make it possible to ask, for the first time, what the cut means in the domain a run was actually conducted in. Nothing can route to a scale while nothing classifies the domain and nothing records it, so the recording is prior to any later change.",
       "DECLARE THE SCALES ONLY and leave the run state as it is, accepting that a finished run cannot afterwards be told which scale it was judged on, so the declaration cannot be audited against anything.",
       "CHANGE NOTHING UNTIL AFTER THE RUNWAY, on the grounds that the cut is operationally inert on the computed severity - moving it from 0.7 to 0.95 reclassifies 0 archived entries - and that the live harm is therefore bounded. This is a defensible reading and its cost is that the 1,416 critical calls already in the archive stay uninterpretable."
     ],
-    "recommendation_standards": "Option 1 is ADDITIVE on both halves: no scale is removed, the 0.7 cut stays exactly where it is, and both changes add a declaration where today there is none - 0 of 20 configurations and 0 of 67 run states. It is the SIMPLEST SUFFICIENT change, because the alternative that would actually bite, replacing the cut, is a change to what the gate admits and is yours rather than mine. It is NOT COMPOSED in the sense your standard requires: the declaration and the recording are independent of one another, no measurement shows the pair dominating either part alone on a named property, and so each stands or falls on its own.",
