   "task:B171": {
+    "title": "B171 - a mid-turn message from him silently discards his own standing discussion marker, so the stop gate prodded past a barrier",
+    "spoken_heading": "WHETHER TO ALLOW THE EDIT TO THE STOP HOOK THAT STOPS YOUR MID-TURN MESSAGES DISCARDING YOUR OWN DISCUSSION MARKER",
+    "what": "The stop gate lists 3 exemptions and its own documentation calls the first one, your instruction to discuss rather than proceed, never overridden. On the 12th of November it refused a stop 9 times in a turn whose message ended with that very instruction. Measured on the live transcript: the gate reads your commands as none. The founder messages in the window carry the discussion marker 3 times and then, fourth, your mid-turn question about a documentation sweep, which carries no command at all. 2 lines of the gate wipe the command set whenever a message of yours arrives after any tool call of mine, so your question wiped your own instruction.",
+    "why": "This is the same fault as the one repaired on the 8th of November arriving through a different door. Then it was your own next line that erased your command, and the repair was to take the union of commands across the turn rather than the last line alone. The union fixed the line path and left the mid-turn message path open. The reset is not wrong in itself, which is why this is not a simple revert: its own comment names the opposite failure it prevents, that a discussion marker from an earlier turn would otherwise silence the gate indefinitely. Both failures are real. The discriminator comes from your own rulings rather than from invention: an unmarked comment that ends in a question mark is a question, and a discussion marker is a barrier rather than an item in a sequence, so answering a question does not lift the barrier.",
     "evidence_line": "Measured on the live transcript on the 12th of November: the gate returns no discussion marker seen, against founder messages carrying it 3 times followed by a question carrying no command. 23 tool calls had run before that question arrived, which is what triggers the reset.",
